  "findings": [
    "The current recruitment page expressly labels its tables as September 2026–2027: ten six-credit first-year courses, ten six-credit second-year courses, and forty-two compulsory third-year credits plus an eighteen-credit minor. Adopted Appendix 1d confirms the same components and credit weights, including four-credit Law/Society and eight-credit thesis. The page’s BSc abbreviation is overridden by Article 30’s LLB qualification. The generic target retains the general programme; the separate Corporate Law specialisation is not imported.",
    "No parent minor is counted with its children. The eighteen-credit space allows the named law minors, an approved outside minor or custom minor; it is not restricted to either named minor. Current Law in Practice contains administrative, private and criminal practice; Sustainable Future contains Foundations of Environmental Law, Private Sector/Sustainability and masterclasses. The transition from the former environmental litigation title is documented in the adopted appendix. The comparator leaves this genuine choice space open.",
    "The recruitment page advertises thirty-credit exchange; Article 35 requires approval for substitutions from an international law school of at least twenty-two EC, with Legal Philosophy B and the thesis completed in Tilburg. The chosen comparator is the standard forty-two-plus-eighteen minor pathway, so no unverified exchange substitution is made. No additional thirty credits or invented host courses are added.",
    "Moot Court requires ninety degree EC at registration, not by the later start or exam. The dated entry confirms that later-earned credits cannot be counted back to registration and describes mandatory attendance, legal-source research, procedural documents and oral pleading. The thesis requires ninety EC when teaching starts, begins in autumn and spans the academic year. Appendix footnote 9 adds a completed non-credit Tilburg Skills Lab for students starting September 2025 or later. Completing Years 1 and 2 and the Year-2 Skills Lab meets both thresholds and the cohort-specific condition.",
    "First-year constitutional law precedes the administrative introduction. Private-law introductions underpin the dedicated Year-2 property, contract and tort courses; substantive/procedural criminal introductions and practical skills underpin later criminal courses. All first-year foundations are completed before Year 2, and both years before the advanced third year. Non-credit Dutch language testing and student-development activities remain outside the 180 credits; the Skills Lab is not optional merely because it is zero-credit.",
    "Read current content for the law courses, philosophical units, Law/Society, comparative legal systems, thesis and moot court. Legal Philosophy B specifically examines EU political/legal union, representation and legitimacy. Law/Society integrates technological, economic and legal-psychological perspectives. Private Law covers property, tort and contract; Criminal Liability covers substantive doctrine; Civil Procedure includes ADR. These actual contents control partial relationships rather than inherited rationales referring to differently named courses from other curricula.",
    "The recruitment page’s stray statement that the thesis concerns a company-law question does not turn the general programme into Corporate Law. The dated thesis entry describes a general independently framed academic legal question with appropriate doctrinal, empirical, comparative or historical methods. No specific topic or compulsory internship is invented. The International Law catalogue entry has little descriptive content; its formal six-credit public-international-law identity and programme outcomes support only a bounded subject-level relationship, not assumed detailed coverage."
